            <ArticleTitle>A placebo-controlled trial of midazolam as an adjunct to morphine patient-controlled analgesia after spinal surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>300-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2013.12.011</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0952-8180(14)00058-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the potential benefit of postoperatively providing a patient-controlled anxiolytic agent, midazolam, in addition to morphine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A double-blinded, placebo-controlled trial of patient-controlled midazolam.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">A Community hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">29 patients undergoing elective spinal surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Postoperatively, via two separate patient-controlled pumps, the treatment group received morphine and midazolam, and the control group received morphine and saline solution.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Repeated measures using numerical rating scales of the primary outcomes of pain and anxiety were obtained every two hours postoperatively. Amount of morphine and midazolam/placebo administered was assessed, as were other secondary outcomes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Anxiety level in the treatment group declined more rapidly over the 24 hours after surgery than in the control group. The treatment group used less morphine than the control. Preoperative positive affect was the only significant psychological predictor of postoperative outcomes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Patients who received both midazolam and morphine experienced a more rapid decline in anxiety and used less opioid medication than those receiving morphine alone.</AbstractText>
